#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143706} :activity/id #uuid "17553f24-c09e-50a0-90d1-43bb87f1c4ba" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050842 :topic/id #uuid "6ae717f0-85c1-5ce8-a9a5-f5bd8976a35e" :topic/math-academy-id 2438} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144079 :tutorial/content "We can use fraction models to divide fractions that have a common denominator. \n\nFor example, suppose we wish to compute the fraction division\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div \\dfrac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's start by writing down a fraction model to represent this division. \n\n![](images/87/87d7afb225bf5f2c70948ef52b7ee815f0bf75040fdb57d7a35dd473adcb24b6.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}4}}{5}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{5}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{5}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}4}{5}.$\n\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}2}$ fits into ${\\color{red}4}.$\n\n- Therefore, instead of $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}4}}{5}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}2}{5}$, we can simply compute ${\\color{red}4}\\div {\\color{blue}2}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}4} \\div {\\color{blue}2} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\div \\dfrac{2}{5} = 2 \\, .\n$$\n\nFinally, looking at our fraction model again, we can see that the shaded parts on the right of the division sign fit exactly twice into the shaded parts on the left. So, our result makes sense." :tutorial/id #uuid "c769890f-1b10-59bb-aafd-9cc2bf0b1939" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8604 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ebf1bb5e-b0bc-58a5-9fb4-22ffe0ab2ee3" :step/math-academy-id 170167 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142610 :question/id #uuid "ae470aa4-327d-5b21-9e11-217710f1ef94" :question/math-academy-id "e-13787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050374}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478447}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "641e303c-47e0-59e4-8e49-4da88bd139f8" :step/math-academy-id 170168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144080 :tutorial/content "We can use similar reasoning to divide fractions with different denominators. The only difference is that we must put the fractions over a common denominator before dividing them.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nA fraction model for this problem is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc6a6b7cf6e21ff22574a832ba99e4ffc9f820a0fb14e0137f66a0d432f65a7d.png)\n\n \n\nOur fractions have the denominators $3$ and $5.$ Therefore, we can make a common denominator of $3\\times 5 = 15.$ \n\nTo create our common denominator, we split the shape on the left of the division sign into $5$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/9d/9d5044383697170194489c492be24c1d91ad87b4fae3a5556718fe6ae56f9965.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{15} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{15}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{5} = \\dfrac{5}{9} \\, .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4255b9e2-eb21-54ba-a3b7-f056ae3ebf16" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8605 :tutorial/title "Dividing Fractions With Different Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "697d3ae3-e066-5421-bd2a-81322ddbb532" :step/math-academy-id 170171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142611 :question/id #uuid "0b51ee70-1d44-5427-8b9f-3930dc3a1962" :question/math-academy-id "e-13790"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050109}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478413}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"} :step/id #uuid "c6d60489-db45-5b87-ab30-b73227a69366" :step/math-academy-id 170172 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142612 :question/id #uuid "daa61f81-4882-5af9-b86a-8b03a586e93b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478457}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"} :step/id #uuid "8e394990-c4a4-5f6b-8dcb-5e355e80c343" :step/math-academy-id 170175 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143711}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142613 :question/id #uuid "066a9329-d63e-55d4-bc6b-bcdb5dd96cc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13793"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478448}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"} :step/id #uuid "3f4d0fbb-6803-5547-aff4-a1a7e37f0cc1" :step/math-academy-id 170178}] :activity/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186143064}]}
